$$-6x + 9y = m$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-15$"},{"key":"B","text":"$15$"},{"key":"C","text":"$-5$"},{"key":"D","text":"$35$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a scalar multiple of the first. Comparing coefficients of $x$: $\\frac{-6}{14} = -\\frac{3}{7}$. Checking $y$: $\\frac{9}{-21} = -\\frac{3}{7}$. The coefficients share the same ratio, so the multiplier is $-\\frac{3}{7}$. For infinitely many solutions, $m$ must equal $-\\frac{3}{7} \\times 35 = -15$. The distractor $15$ results from computing the correct magnitude but dropping the negative sign. The distractor $-5$ results from dividing 35 by 7 and applying the negative sign but omitting the factor of 3 in the numerator, reflecting incomplete reasoning about the scalar multiple. The distractor $35$ results from assuming the constants must be identical across both equations, ignoring the scaling factor entirely.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





