$$4x - 9y = -51$$', NULL, $lyceon_json$[]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, '7', ARRAY['7'], E'Subtracting the second equation from the first eliminates $x$: $(4x + 9y) - (4x - 9y) = 75 - (-51)$, giving $18y = 126$, so $y = 7$. A common error is adding the equations instead of subtracting, which gives $8x = 24$ and $x = 3$, solving for $x$ rather than $y$. Another error is computing $18y = 126$ correctly but dividing by 9 instead of 18, yielding an incorrect value of 14.', NULL, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Behavioral ecologist Sonia Petrescu conducted a field study in which she observed that European bee-eaters nesting in colonies of fewer than fifteen pairs experienced a predation rate on eggs and nestlings nearly three times higher than those in colonies exceeding forty pairs. Petrescu concluded that larger colony size confers a direct protective benefit, likely through enhanced collective vigilance — more adult birds scanning for predators means earlier detection and more effective mobbing responses.

Text 2
Ornithologist Kwame Asante analyzed habitat data for seventy-one European bee-eater colonies across the Iberian Peninsula and found that colony size strongly correlated with nesting-site quality: larger colonies consistently occupied south-facing riverbank cliffs with harder substrate and fewer access points for terrestrial predators, while smaller colonies were disproportionately found on exposed, easily accessible embankments. Asante''s regression analysis showed that when substrate hardness and the number of ground-level access points were controlled for, colony size alone explained less than eight percent of the variation in predation rates.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Asante's data suggest that the lower predation rates Petrescu attributed to collective vigilance may instead result from the superior physical characteristics of sites that happen to attract larger colonies"},{"key":"B","text":"Asante confirms Petrescu's conclusion that collective vigilance is the primary mechanism reducing predation in larger colonies"},{"key":"C","text":"Asante demonstrates that European bee-eaters do not actually nest in colonies, undermining the observational basis of Petrescu's study"},{"key":"D","text":"Asante's regression analysis proves that colony size has absolutely no effect on predation rates under any conditions"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Petrescu observed lower predation in larger colonies and inferred that collective vigilance was the cause. Asante''s habitat analysis introduces a confounding variable: larger colonies occupy physically safer sites with harder substrate and fewer predator access points. When these site characteristics are statistically controlled, colony size explains very little of the predation variation, suggesting that the protective effect Petrescu attributed to group behavior may actually stem from habitat quality. Confirming the collective vigilance conclusion directly contradicts Asante''s findings, which show colony size loses most of its explanatory power once site quality is accounted for. Demonstrating that bee-eaters do not nest in colonies is factually wrong within the passage; Asante''s study analyzes seventy-one colonies, fully accepting colonial nesting as an established behavior. Proving that colony size has absolutely no effect overstates Asante''s findings; his regression shows colony size explains less than eight percent of the variation, which is a very small effect, not a zero effect.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


- Many historians have assumed that medieval European blacksmiths worked exclusively with locally sourced iron ore, given the high cost and logistical difficulty of long-distance transport in the period.
- Metallurgist Elif Karasu analyzed the trace-element compositions of 62 iron artifacts recovered from a twelfth-century smithy in central Anatolia.
- Karasu found that 23 of the 62 artifacts contained phosphorus and arsenic ratios that do not match any known Anatolian ore deposit but are consistent with iron ore deposits in the Ural Mountains, over 2,500 kilometers to the northeast.
- The artifacts with non-local ore signatures included both utilitarian tools (plowshares, hinges) and higher-value items (sword blanks, decorative fittings).
- Karasu published her findings in the 2023 issue of the Journal of Archaeological Science.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Elif Karasu's trace-element analysis of 62 iron artifacts from a twelfth-century Anatolian smithy, published in the Journal of Archaeological Science in 2023, examined both utilitarian tools and higher-value items such as sword blanks."},{"key":"B","text":"Among the iron artifacts Karasu studied, 23 contained phosphorus and arsenic ratios matching Ural Mountain ore deposits rather than local Anatolian sources, and these non-local items included everyday tools alongside prestige objects."},{"key":"C","text":"Karasu's discovery that 23 of 62 artifacts from a twelfth-century Anatolian smithy contain trace-element signatures matching Ural Mountain ore -- over 2,500 kilometers away -- undermines the widespread assumption that medieval blacksmiths relied solely on locally sourced iron."},{"key":"D","text":"Historians have generally believed that medieval European blacksmiths used only local iron ore because transporting heavy materials over long distances was prohibitively expensive and logistically difficult during the period."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to present evidence that challenges a commonly held assumption. The choice identifying the specific evidence (23 of 62 artifacts matching Ural Mountain ore signatures from 2,500 kilometers away) and explicitly framing it as undermining the assumption of exclusively local sourcing accomplishes both elements: it states the evidence and positions it against the assumption. The choice about Karasu''s 2023 publication describes the study''s scope and the types of artifacts analyzed but does not identify the assumption being challenged or explain how the findings undermine it. The choice about phosphorus and arsenic ratios reports the data and notes the mix of utilitarian and prestige items but does not frame the findings as challenging any assumption. The choice about historians'' belief explains the assumption itself but presents no evidence against it.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Archaeologist Petra Lindqvist argues that the abandonment of several Norse farmsteads in western Greenland around 1350 was driven primarily by a catastrophic decline in walrus ivory trade. Lindqvist''s analysis of middens at three abandoned sites shows a sharp drop in walrus bone fragments after 1280, coinciding with the period when cheaper elephant ivory from newly established African trade routes flooded European markets, eliminating the primary economic incentive for maintaining remote Greenland settlements.

Text 2
Paleoclimatologist Yousef Hadid reconstructed summer temperatures in western Greenland using lake sediment cores spanning 800 to 1500 CE. Hadid''s data show that average July temperatures dropped by approximately 2.7 degrees Celsius between 1280 and 1350, rendering the already marginal growing season too short to sustain the cattle and sheep herds that Norse settlers depended on for dairy and wool. Hadid notes that this cooling trend persisted for over a century and was not a brief fluctuation.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Text 2 offers an alternative explanation for the same historical event that Text 1 attributes to economic factors"},{"key":"B","text":"Text 2 provides additional evidence supporting Lindqvist's walrus ivory trade thesis"},{"key":"C","text":"Text 2 argues that the Norse farmsteads were never actually abandoned, contradicting Text 1's premise"},{"key":"D","text":"Text 2 examines a different geographic region than the one discussed in Text 1"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Both passages address the abandonment of Norse farmsteads in western Greenland around 1350, but they propose different primary causes: Lindqvist points to the collapse of the walrus ivory trade, while Hadid identifies severe climate cooling that undermined agricultural viability. Providing additional support for the ivory trade thesis mischaracterizes Hadid''s research, which presents an independent climatic explanation rather than evidence reinforcing an economic one. Arguing that the farmsteads were never abandoned contradicts the shared premise of both passages, which take the abandonment as an established fact and seek to explain it. Examining a different geographic region is factually incorrect, as both passages explicitly discuss western Greenland during the same approximate time period.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);















- The olm (Proteus anguinus) is a fully aquatic salamander that lives exclusively in the underground cave systems of the Dinaric Alps, a limestone karst region spanning parts of Slovenia, Croatia, and Bosnia and Herzegovina.
- The olm is entirely blind, having evolved vestigial eyes covered by skin, and relies on chemical and electrical sensory receptors to navigate and find prey.
- Adults typically measure between 25 and 30 centimeters in length and can survive without food for up to 10 years by drastically slowing their metabolism.
- The species was first scientifically described in 1768 by the Austrian naturalist Josephus Nicolaus Laurenti.
- Conservation efforts have focused on protecting the karst aquifers from agricultural runoff, which can introduce nitrates and pesticides into the underground water systems the olm inhabits.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"First described in 1768 by Josephus Nicolaus Laurenti, the olm has vestigial eyes covered by skin and navigates cave environments using chemical and electrical sensory receptors."},{"key":"B","text":"The olm is a fully aquatic salamander found exclusively in the underground cave systems of the Dinaric Alps, a limestone karst region that stretches across parts of Slovenia, Croatia, and Bosnia and Herzegovina."},{"key":"C","text":"Conservation programs for the olm have prioritized shielding Dinaric Alps aquifers from agricultural runoff containing nitrates and pesticides that threaten the species' underground habitat."},{"key":"D","text":"Capable of surviving up to 10 years without food by drastically reducing its metabolism, the olm typically reaches 25 to 30 centimeters in length as an adult."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to identify what the organism is and explain where it lives. The choice describing the olm as a fully aquatic salamander found exclusively in the Dinaric Alps'' underground cave systems accomplishes both objectives -- it identifies the organism''s type and specifies its habitat and geographic range. The choice about Laurenti''s 1768 description identifies sensory adaptations and the historical describer but does not explain where the olm lives. The choice about conservation programs discusses threats to the habitat but does not identify what the olm is. The choice about metabolism and body length describes physical and physiological traits but provides no information about what type of organism the olm is or where it lives.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);








- Neuroscientist Clara Vidal (2021) used functional MRI to study how trained musicians process unexpected chord progressions in Western tonal music.
- Vidal found that hearing an unexpected chord triggered increased activation in the anterior insula, a brain region associated with detecting prediction errors -- mismatches between what the brain expects and what it actually perceives.
- Cognitive scientist Ryota Matsumoto (2022) used electroencephalography (EEG) to measure brain responses when participants viewed visual sequences in which the final image violated an established pattern.
- Matsumoto found that pattern-violating images produced a strong neural signal originating in the anterior insula, consistent with a prediction-error response.
- Both researchers concluded that the anterior insula functions as a domain-general prediction-error detector, responding to expectation violations regardless of whether the input is auditory or visual.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Vidal used functional MRI to study musicians' responses to unexpected chords in 2021, while Matsumoto used EEG to examine responses to pattern-violating images in 2022, with both studies focusing on how the brain processes surprising stimuli."},{"key":"B","text":"The anterior insula, a brain region linked to prediction-error detection, showed increased activation in Vidal's study of unexpected chord progressions and produced a strong neural signal in Matsumoto's study of visual pattern violations, suggesting the region is involved in processing both auditory and visual information."},{"key":"C","text":"Working independently with different neuroimaging methods and different sensory domains -- Vidal studying auditory surprises in music and Matsumoto studying visual pattern violations -- both researchers traced expectation-violation responses to the same structure, the anterior insula, supporting its role as a domain-general prediction-error detector."},{"key":"D","text":"Matsumoto's 2022 EEG study found that images violating an established visual pattern produced a strong anterior insula signal, a result that complemented Vidal's earlier fMRI finding of anterior insula activation during unexpected musical chord progressions."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize that two independent studies investigating different sensory modalities converge on the same underlying neurological mechanism. The choice specifying that the researchers worked independently with different methods and different sensory domains, then stating they both traced responses to the anterior insula and interpreting this convergence as evidence for its domain-general role, accomplishes all three elements of the goal: independence, different modalities, and shared mechanism. The choice mentioning fMRI and EEG describes the different methods and notes both studies concern surprising stimuli but does not identify the shared neurological mechanism or emphasize convergence on a specific brain structure. The choice about anterior insula activation names the shared structure and notes both sensory domains but frames the finding as the region being "involved in processing both auditory and visual information" rather than emphasizing the convergence of independent studies on a common mechanism. The choice presenting Matsumoto''s result as complementing Vidal''s frames the relationship as sequential confirmation rather than independent convergence, and it does not articulate the domain-general mechanism the essay aims to highlight.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

Marine biologist Ji-won Park recommends establishing large no-fishing reserves covering at least thirty percent of continental shelf waters to allow depleted fish populations to recover. Park''s five-year tracking study of a reserve off the coast of Jeju Island showed that biomass of commercially targeted species inside the reserve increased by sixty-one percent, with measurable spillover benefits to adjacent fishing grounds.

Text 2
Fisheries economist Tendai Nkomo studied eighteen no-fishing reserves across the western Indian Ocean and found that while fish populations inside reserves consistently recovered, the economic impact on small-scale fishing communities was severe. In eleven of the eighteen cases, displaced fishers concentrated their efforts in the remaining open waters, intensifying pressure on those areas and reducing their overall catch by an average of twenty-three percent within three years. Nkomo argues that reserves designed without concurrent economic support programs for displaced fishers risk redistributing ecological harm rather than eliminating it.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By warning that reserves may shift fishing pressure to unprotected waters and harm fishing communities unless economic support is provided alongside the reserves"},{"key":"B","text":"By agreeing that thirty percent coverage is the ideal target and urging its immediate adoption across all continental shelves"},{"key":"C","text":"By disputing that fish populations recover inside no-fishing reserves"},{"key":"D","text":"By recommending that all commercial fishing be permanently banned worldwide"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Nkomo''s research shows that reserves can displace fishing effort into remaining open waters, worsening outcomes for both ecosystems and communities. She would likely caution Park that establishing reserves without economic support programs risks redistributing harm rather than preventing it. Agreeing with the thirty percent target contradicts Nkomo''s findings, which highlight serious unintended consequences of reserves implemented without accompanying economic measures. Disputing that fish populations recover directly contradicts Nkomo''s own data, which confirms that populations inside reserves consistently recovered. Recommending a permanent worldwide ban on fishing is an extreme position far beyond anything Nkomo''s research suggests; her argument calls for economic support alongside reserves, not the elimination of fishing.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




